{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2875 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8d3757fa395a56ccd3bc816b66c564d91ef75cab81656a1840744b99c0780d1d" :edb/db-before-t 2874 :edb/db-after-t 2875 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536187 50 #inst "2026-10-09T18:49:06.863Z" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198003 1100 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}$." 13194139536187 false] [17592186198003 1100 "Find the points of the intersection of the elliptic cone $x^2 + y^2=z^2$ and the line $x +1=y-1 =\\dfrac{z}{2}.$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198003 1145 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n\n$$\n{x = t - 1; y = t + 1; z = 2t\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + y^{2} &= z^{2} \\\\\n(t - 1)^{2} + (t + 1)^{2} &= (2t)^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} - 2t + 1 + t^{2} + 2t + 1 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2t^{2} + 2 &= 4t^{2} \\\\\n2 &= 2t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nt &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n${x =-1 - 1 =-2; y =-1 + 1 = 0; z = 2(-1) =-2$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right)$.\n- For $t=1$, we have\n${x = 1 - 1 = 0; y = 1 + 1 = 2; z = 2(1) = 2$,\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2)$." 13194139536187 false] [17592186198003 1145 "First, we write the equation of our line in the parametric form:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14083596/assets/41e6a96cc57d9ce57d5a7bec35586175d9217f92e4dc2bab106db641db9f24ea.png)\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nSubstituting the above expressions for $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ into the equation of the elliptic cone, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ (t-1)^{2}+(t+1)^{2} & =(2t)^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t+1+{t}^{2}+2t+1 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2{t}^{2}+2 & =4{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1 \\\\ t & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-1=-2 \\\\ y=-1+1=0 \\\\ z=2(-1)=-2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,0,-2\\right).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-1=0 \\\\ y=1+1=2 \\\\ z=2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(0,2,2\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,0,-2)$ and $(0,2,2).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198155 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line\n${x = 4t; y = 8t,; z = 2t$\nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$." 13194139536187 false] [17592186198155 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\dfrac{x^2}{24} + \\dfrac{y^2}{24} +\\dfrac{z^2}{6} = 1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=4t \\\\ y=8t, \\\\ z=2t\\end{aligned}$ \nwhere $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198155 1145 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{24} + \\frac{y^{2}}{24} + \\frac{z^{2}}{6} &= 1 \\\\\nx^{2} + y^{2} + 4z^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n(4t)^{2} + (8t)^{2} + 4(2t)^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n16t^{2} + 64t^{2} + 16t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\n96t^{2} &= 24 \\\\\nt^{2} &= \\frac{1}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution\n\n$$\nt = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-2; y = 8(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-4; z = 2(-\\frac{1}{2}) =-1$,\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right)$.\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2$, we have\n${x = 4(\\frac{1}{2}) = 2; y = 8(\\frac{1}{2}) = 4; z = 2(\\frac{1}{2}) = 1$,\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1)$." 13194139536187 false] [17592186198155 1145 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =24 \\\\ (4t)^{2}+(8t)^{2}+4(2t)^{2} & =24 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}+64{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ 96{t}^{2} & =24 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solution $t = \\pm \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(-\\frac{1}{2})=-2 \\\\ y=8(-\\frac{1}{2})=-4 \\\\ z=2(-\\frac{1}{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $\\left(-2,-4,-1\\right).$\n\n- For $t=\\dfrac 1 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=4(\\frac{1}{2})=2 \\\\ y=8(\\frac{1}{2})=4 \\\\ z=2(\\frac{1}{2})=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $\\left(2,4,1\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-2,-4,-1)$ and $(2,4,1).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198156 1145 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x$. First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\nx - z = 3⟹x = z + 3\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(((z + 3) + 1)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n((z + 4)^{2})/(16) + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2}}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{2z^{2} + 8z + 16}{16} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + \\frac{z^{2} + 4z + 8}{8} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z^{2} + 4z + 4) + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2} + 4)/(8) &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) + \\frac{1}{2} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{4} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(8) &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{2} + ((z + 2)^{2})/(4) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1\n$$" 13194139536187 false] [17592186198156 1145 "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-z=3\\,⟹\\,x=z+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{((z+3)+1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(z+4)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{2{z}^{2}+8z+16}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}+4z+8}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{({z}^{2}+4z+4)+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}+4}{8} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\dfrac{y^2}{2}+\\dfrac{(z+2)^2}{4} = 1.$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198273 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387834 13194139536187 true] [17592186198273 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387841 13194139536187 true] [17592186198273 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387862 13194139536187 true] [17592186198273 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387876 13194139536187 true] [17592186198281 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387855 13194139536187 true] [17592186198281 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387890 13194139536187 true] [17592186198281 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387897 13194139536187 true] [17592186198281 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387904 13194139536187 true] [17592186198281 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387925 13194139536187 true] [17592186198360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387848 13194139536187 true] [17592186198360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387869 13194139536187 true] [17592186198360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387883 13194139536187 true] [17592186198360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387911 13194139536187 true] [17592186198360 1020 #edb/ref 17592186387918 13194139536187 true] [17592186198920 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536187 true] [17592186198920 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} + \\frac{z^{2}}{4} = 1$ and the line\n\n${x = t; y = 2t,; z = t$ where $t ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$." 13194139536187 false] [17592186198920 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=t \\\\ y=2t, \\\\ z=t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186198920 1145 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the ellipsoid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ 8{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8(t)^{2}+(2t)^{2}+4(t)^{2} & =16 \\\\ 8{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2}+4{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-1$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(-1)=-1 \\\\ y=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ z=(-1)=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(-1,-2,-1).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(1)=1 \\\\ y=2(1)=2 \\\\ z=(1)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(1,2,1).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(-1,-2,-1)$ and $(1,2,1).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387835 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1089 "q-70954" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=-t \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}t, \\\\ z=t+2\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1113 #uuid "fce347c2-ad04-51e4-8225-7d5f64f3611a" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387834 1145 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {(-t)}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+{(t+2)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {t}^{2}+2{t}^{2}+({t}^{2}+4t+4) & =4 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4t+4 & =4 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+4t & =0 \\\\ t(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=-1$ and $t=0.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=-1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-(-1)=1 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}(-1)=-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=(-1)+2=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(1,-\\sqrt{2},1).$\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=(0)=0 \\\\ y=\\sqrt{2}(0)=0 \\\\ z=(0)+2=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(0,0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(1,-\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387836 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387837 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387838 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387839 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387840 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1041 "selection" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387836 13194139536187 true] [17592186387835 1110 #uuid "99060d62-00f9-59f5-9390-0e9fdf030737" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387836 1034 "$(1,-\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2)$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387836 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387836 1111 #uuid "ef74b440-3cfb-5480-98bd-c857f8fbd7dc" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387837 1034 "$(-\\sqrt{2},1,-1)$ and $(2,0,0)$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387837 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387837 1111 #uuid "a223fe25-1337-5831-a52c-1da18146a174" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387838 1034 "$(-2,-\\sqrt{2},2)$ and $(-1,0,\\sqrt{2})$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387838 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387838 1111 #uuid "7e1bf8bb-1644-5d7f-b2b9-5d4ffd450d5d" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387839 1034 "$(1,\\sqrt{2},3)$ and $(1,2\\sqrt{2},2)$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387839 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387839 1111 #uuid "aa0b41ac-8743-584d-aeda-360890155d50" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387840 1034 "$(-1,-2\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(0,0,2)$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387840 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387840 1111 #uuid "1a5ff533-06dd-58df-80fb-3e67794d92d7" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1017 #edb/ref 17592186387842 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1089 "q-70955" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1100 "Find the point(s) of the intersection of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ and the line \n${\\begin{aligned}x=2t+2 \\\\ y=2t-4\\,, \\\\ z=-2\\sqrt{2}t\\end{aligned}$ where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1113 #uuid "a259cb2b-e950-5824-9397-ca2ca9ba3f74" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387841 1145 "Substituting the expressions for $x$, $y$, and $z$ from the parametric equation of the line into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {((2t+2)-2)}^{2}+{(2t-4)}^{2}+{(-2\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 4{t}^{2}+(4{t}^{2}-16t+16)+8{t}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}-16t+16 & =16 \\\\ 16{t}^{2}-16t & =0 \\\\ t(t-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $t=0$ and $t=1.$ \n\nNow, we substitute our solutions back into the equations of the line.\n\n- For $t=0$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(0)+2=2 \\\\ y=2(0)-4=-4 \\\\ z=-2\\sqrt{2}(0)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the first point is $(2,-4,0).$\n\n- For $t=1$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=2(1)+2=4 \\\\ y=2(1)-4=-2 \\\\ z=-2\\sqrt{2}(1)=-2\\sqrt{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the second point is $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are $(2,-4,0)$ and $(4,-2,-2\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387843 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387844 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387845 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387846 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1000 #edb/ref 17592186387847 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1041 "selection" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1104 #edb/ref 17592186387846 13194139536187 true] [17592186387842 1110 #uuid "7c0fefb9-4da4-5721-ba08-d20540d3d103" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387843 1034 "$(2,0,-\\sqrt{2})$ and $(6,0,-4)$" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387843 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387843 1111 #uuid "caefd944-7804-5d1e-a7b0-81a84c0998da" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387844 1034 "$(2,-4,0)$ only" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387844 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536187 true] [17592186387844 1111 #uuid "b2f03d6a-8a77-579c-98bb-7f3eb153cb64" 13194139536187 true] [17592186387845 1034 "$(-2,0,-4)$ and $(0,6,-4\\sqrt{2})$" 13194139536187 true] [175921863878